  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA strong cold front will approach from the west today as low \npressure tracks into the St. Lawrence River Valley. For early \nthis morning, a warm front will approach from the southwest with\nbands of rain passing through ahead of the front at the leading\nedge of a strengthening low level jet. Strong forcing and some \nelevated instability will allow for some locally heavy downpours\nand rumbles of thunder to occur toward and shortly after \ndaybreak, especially across the Mohawk Valley and southern \nAdirondacks, Capital/Saratoga and Lake George Region into \nsouthern VT. Rainfall rates of 0.25-0.50\"/hour could lead to \nsome ponding of water in poor drainage areas for the morning \ncommute in these areas.\n\nAs the low level jet translates overhead around and shortly\nafter daybreak, strong south to southeast winds will be possible\nacross portions of the Capital Region and within some\nnorth/south oriented valleys. Latest REFs suggest some gusts of\n30-40 mph could occur in these areas through mid morning before\ndecreasing as the core of the low level jet passes by and \nweakens.\n\nAs the cold front approaches from the west during the afternoon\nhours, some surface-based destabilization should occur, \nespecially if some breaks of sun can develop. Latest HREFs and \nREFs suggest SB CAPES of 100-250 J/kg developing for areas \nmainly south of I-90. Strengthening mid level winds will allow \n0-6 km shear to increase to 50-60 KT amid this instability, \nparallel to the incoming front. This suggests the possibility of\na low topped line of showers/thunderstorms to develop which \ncould produce a few instances of damaging wind gusts. SPC has \nexpanded the Marginal Risk (Level 1 of 5) for severe \nthunderstorms northward, now encompassing much of the Mohawk\nValley, Capital Region and southern VT, with the Slight Risk\n(Level 2 of 5) expanding just slightly north into portions of\nGreene/Columbia counties. Even in the absence of convection, as\nthe front passes through, some brief wind gusts of 35-45 mph \nwill be possible this evening.\n\nIn the wake of the front, blustery conditions will develop\ntonight and continue into Saturday with northwest winds gusting\n25-35 mph, perhaps a bit stronger Saturday morning as mixing \ndeepens. Some lingering moisture in the wake of the front, \npartially from Lake Effect, will allow for some snow showers \nacross higher terrain areas of the southern Adirondacks and \nsouthern VT late tonight into Saturday morning, where some \ncoatings to less than one half inch of snow accumulation will be\npossible. A few flurries/sprinkles could even occur across the \neastern Catskills, the Berkshires, and at lower elevations such \nas the Mohawk Valley and Capital Region Saturday morning. High \ntemperatures Saturday should only reach the 30s to lower 40s \nacross higher elevations, with mainly 40s for valley regions. \nCombined with brisk winds, wind chill temperatures (\"feels-like\"\ntemperatures) will be in the 20s/lower 30s for higher terrain, \nand 30s in valleys through much of the day.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAfter an unseasonably cold morning Sunday with low temps mainly\nin the 20s, temperatures should moderate into the 50s for most\nareas by Sunday afternoon. Temperatures will continue to\nmoderate to seasonable levels early next week with highs\nreaching the upper 50s to lower 60s in valleys. Generally dry\nconditions are expected through Wednesday.",
